= Wave 14 context capture =

'''State: Phase 1 — Context capture.'''

Wave 14 resolves the final “miscellaneous durable seeds” slot by promoting a real semantic cluster rather than creating an unrelated grab bag: scholarly communication, publication, open science, and research-data stewardship.

== Normalized scope ==

# [[Scientific literature]]
# [[Scholarly communication]]
# [[Peer review]]
# [[Citation]]
# [[Academic publishing]]
# [[Preprint]]
# [[Open access]]
# [[Open science]]
# [[Dataset]]
# [[Research data management]]
# [[Data repository]]
# [[FAIR data]]

== Why this is a coherent wave ==

The cluster joins four already-developed parts of BITwiki:

* [[Evidence]], [[Provenance]], [[Verification]], [[Validation]], [[Reproducibility]], and [[Replication]];
* [[Metadata]], [[Database]], [[Knowledge representation]], and [[Semantic Web]];
* [[Scientific method]] and [[Scientific model]];
* [[Bioinformatics]], [[Biological database]], and [[Reproducible computational research]].

The missing layer is how research outputs are communicated, evaluated, cited, published, opened, preserved, and made reusable.

== Title decisions ==

* [[Scientific literature]] refers to the body of scientific research communication, not literature as fiction or the humanities generally.
* [[Scholarly communication]] is broader than publication: it includes creation, evaluation, dissemination, discovery, preservation, and reuse.
* [[Academic publishing]] is retained as a process/institutional layer inside scholarly communication.
* [[Preprint]] is a public manuscript version not yet certified by journal/conference peer review; later versions may exist.
* [[Dataset]] is broader than a database and may be static or versioned.
* [[FAIR data]] uses the established acronym because the FAIR Guiding Principles are a named research-data framework.
* [[Data repository]] is distinct from [[Database]]: a repository emphasizes deposit, preservation, discovery, access, and stewardship of research objects.

== Domain decision ==

No new controlled Domain is introduced. Wave 14 uses Science, Computer science, Technology, and Systems science where appropriate.

== Dependency graph ==

<pre>
Scholarly communication
 ├→ Scientific literature
 │    ├→ Citation
 │    └→ Peer review
 ├→ Academic publishing
 │    └→ Preprint
 └→ Open access
      └→ Open science

Research-data layer
 Dataset
 ├→ Research data management
 │    └→ Data repository
 └→ FAIR data
</pre>

This graph is explanatory, not a strict subclass hierarchy.

== Required non-equivalences ==

<pre>
scientific literature ≠ all scientific knowledge
scholarly communication ≠ academic publishing only
peer review ≠ proof of correctness
citation ≠ endorsement
citation count ≠ quality
academic publishing ≠ peer review
preprint ≠ peer-reviewed article
open access ≠ open science
free-to-read ≠ unrestricted reuse necessarily
open science ≠ all research must be public without ethical/legal limits
dataset ≠ database
dataset ≠ raw data necessarily
research data management ≠ data sharing only
data repository ≠ database
repository deposit ≠ preservation guarantee
FAIR ≠ open
FAIR ≠ data quality
FAIR ≠ one implementation standard
</pre>

== Source families ==

* National Academies / publication-ethics literature — peer review and research evaluation.
* Crossref / DataCite / scholarly metadata practice — citations, identifiers, scholarly record relationships.
* NIH/NLM — preprints and current data-management/sharing practice.
* UNESCO Recommendation on Open Science (2021) — open-science scope and open-access context.
* Budapest/Berlin open-access declarations and UNESCO guidance — open-access definitions.
* Wilkinson et al. (2016) FAIR Guiding Principles — findability, accessibility, interoperability, reusability.
* NIH Data Management and Sharing Policy plus 2026 plan-format updates — current research-data management and repository practice.

== Critical current-state notes ==

* NIH's 2023 Data Management and Sharing Policy remains in force; NIH introduced a simplified required DMS Plan format for 2026 rather than replacing the underlying policy.
* FAIR is explicitly not equivalent to open: controlled-access data can be FAIR when access conditions and metadata are properly described.
* UNESCO's Recommendation on Open Science was adopted 23 November 2021 and treats open science as broader than open access.

== Future normalized redlinks ==

Journal, conference proceeding, monograph, bibliographic database, reference list, persistent identifier, Digital Object Identifier, data citation, data paper, supplementary material, editorial review, registered report, postprint, institutional repository, subject repository, data stewardship, data curation, research object, research software citation, open peer review.

These are coverage signals, not authorization for stubs.

